#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f0ec6b-9cb4-5246-9ac6-23b35e3b224e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of More Than Two Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "e47a8da7-9276-5f4c-8ee6-1fd737c2e9ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4277" :question/problem "Consider the diagram shown below, where $\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{PT}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\overrightarrow{QS}=\\mathbf{c}.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{TS}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/04ce04bf7c7f88d6578c74db0d7398a6d299d11bd9f60f4fc4b6e1bfb0129f3b.png)" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $S$ consisting only of the vectors $\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b,$ or $\\mathbf c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\overrightarrow{TS}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{TP}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{PQ}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{QS}}}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/53940607b5cbf0220008e6e31ec60f2fd7fd2e4f70c5a8e95e7fcfd64064b095.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{TP}} = -\\mathbf{b}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{PQ}} = \\mathbf{a}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{QS}} = \\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/810cce10eb330b1767a101d9107a9534c437daa123f13056a486a9fc96dbab89.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TS} & =\\overset{\\to }{TP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QS} \\\\  & =-b+a+c \\\\  & =a-b+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/id #uuid "0b0dbbc1-d39d-5aff-9f16-075779d36659" :question/math-academy-id "e-5015" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/2420154d44b786c6aad54212760e9a37ce67dbdce94a7043039f0e824c5ef9bc.png)\n\nIn parallelogram $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{CB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}=\\overrightarrow{CA}$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{CB}-\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\overrightarrow{AC}$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{DC}=\\overrightarrow{BA}$" :question/worked-solution "- Equality I is true. Indeed, $\\overrightarrow{CB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}=\\overrightarrow{CA}.$\n\n- Equality II is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CB}-\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BA} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CA} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{AC}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nsince $\\overrightarrow{CA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ have opposite directions.\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed, $\\overrightarrow{DC}\\neq\\overrightarrow{BA}$ since they have opposite directions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c43200-991b-5854-be1d-fa9db80f3725" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of Two Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "c7526e18-37a9-572a-a6a3-0384bbccf41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9934" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/7431bd3281e4e49f4caaa5b701885fb64c58196b2256af9d8744decd9a0d0793.png)\n\n  \n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overrightarrow{PS}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{QS}=\\mathbf{b},$ $\\overrightarrow{PT}=\\mathbf{c},$ and $\\overrightarrow{TS}=\\mathbf{d}.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{TQ}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c},$ and $\\mathbf{d}.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b, \\mathbf c,$ and $\\mathbf d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition, \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\overrightarrow{TQ}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{TS}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/4902397acd28e2e5f85063ba55c576d3768360225a705f31a66bd460405dd5a6.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{TS}} = \\mathbf{d}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}} = -\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/08086f7e09568f908aa4919a1156d52e465216221be8908e72a1fd90ec8843dd.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{TS}+\\overset{\\to }{SQ} \\\\  & =d+(-b) \\\\  & =d-b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c43200-991b-5854-be1d-fa9db80f3725" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of Two Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4ab85c3-982b-5822-b7dc-065cdd290e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{XW}=a+b-c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379739} {:answer/id #uuid "32612b0a-1a09-5e06-b7a5-f9fd97ce4874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{XW}=d-a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379740} {:answer/id #uuid "e285424f-a347-5c34-b441-5a2fa3dddd16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{XW}=d+a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379741} {:answer/id #uuid "69d82a70-6f5e-559b-80c5-061c48c1387e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{XW}=c-a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379742} {:answer/id #uuid "9a839e87-45d3-5fd3-af3f-ca0e257c81f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{XW}=a-b-c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32612b0a-1a09-5e06-b7a5-f9fd97ce4874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{XW}=d-a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379740} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3ac349-25c9-587d-86f6-7723acdad4ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b50f56b2-17cd-5b80-9652-579aebb80ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-11323" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/36aaf58af536428c74cdb8f4c02c2b4f07e414422e8aea378c75d7abcff58cff.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{YX}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{XV}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{VZ}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{YW}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{XW}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $X$ to $W$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$  \n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{XW}=\\overset{\\to }{XY}+\\overset{\\to }{YW},\n$$\n\nas shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/4758b51fd3dc076059d60e944de0ad8375bb304240365959d4c6cae94f95a535.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{XY}=-a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{YW}=d.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/e942d61c1f51c36dc6cd04f88ff5e37f538920a34c1af5bc780c47691567b668.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{XW} & =\\overset{\\to }{XY}+\\overset{\\to }{YW} \\\\  & =-a+d \\\\  & =d-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7483a2ea-b906-5d1b-8c9b-328f61e8034e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379746} {:answer/id #uuid "4725536b-6b1b-5be8-9715-0498f0f36934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379747} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b5d8d6-601e-59b9-93d5-50d48142d403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379748} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4fb9cb-d38f-500f-a042-697dc8f27d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379749} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0fe0b2-696e-5618-a8f7-1a99a4c51e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c0fe0b2-696e-5618-a8f7-1a99a4c51e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379750} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba0e5314-b4c8-5498-a0a5-855783a18840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "712c5fd8-f058-5907-ae23-eef7cffb0176" :question/math-academy-id "q-118976" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/dcd36221e7ad293f0aac1ab99ece80641cb077efd05adc168a552e97d86100c8.png)\n\nIn parallelogram $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AD}=\\overset{\\to }{CB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}=\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\overset{\\to }{FA}$" :question/worked-solution "- Equality I is false. Indeed, $\\overset{\\to }{AD}\\ne \\overset{\\to }{CB}$ since they have the opposite directions.\n\n- Equality II is true. Indeed, $\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}=\\overset{\\to }{DB}.$\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed, $\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\overset{\\to }{AF}\\ne \\overset{\\to }{FA}$ since they have the opposite directions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c43200-991b-5854-be1d-fa9db80f3725" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of Two Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8694da7-b586-5d35-9e72-c4e8f7936822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=b-c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379753} {:answer/id #uuid "f39fd815-feed-572c-a0b6-9ae9f684e84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=b+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379754} {:answer/id #uuid "1618f7e9-c970-51b4-8227-5ba1ba2aaa98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a-b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379755} {:answer/id #uuid "5d78843a-80b1-5086-b6b7-8d6c3eecae3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a+b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379756} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbec6e8-e27d-5b9b-96b6-30c815733db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a+d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1618f7e9-c970-51b4-8227-5ba1ba2aaa98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a-b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379755} :answer-field/id #uuid "391684fd-6aaf-504b-abb5-cba6b417dddd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b958283a-525c-51ab-a33e-68049ee3df0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-12049" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/2f9e6eff196e24ddabca8d778d27e499fc1b856ef7169257a31a7cbaca8b564b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{TP}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RS}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{QT}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $T$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QT}=\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PT},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/e1d5eb2f392332f69fa1507899b30b838a42ebd1c40dacddeca4edcf0f7aa8c2.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PT}=-b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/0e8e24968141284550fac7d4e5c63333789f30ade8f7d5dd8b87fae4e012673a.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QT} & =\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PT} \\\\  & =a+(-b) \\\\  & =a-b\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f0ec6b-9cb4-5246-9ac6-23b35e3b224e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of More Than Two Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6114259-afda-5a96-be85-beb90e90e47d" :answer/type 